Introduction
Registan Square, meaning "Sandy Place," was the heart of medieval Samarkand, serving as a hub for commerce, culture, and education. Framed by three monumental madrasahs—Ulugbek, Sher‑Dor, and Tilla‑Kari—built between the 15th and 17th centuries, it is a UNESCO World Heritage site that exemplifies the pinnacle of Central Asian architecture.
